sum='King Bruce of Scotland had tried again and again to do a great deed to make his people glad, but he failed every time. Sad and lonely, he flung himself down in low despair and decided, “I’ll give it all up.” Just then a spider dropped down on its silken thread. Its cobweb home was far up near the ceiling dome. The spider began to climb, but slipped and fell. It ran up again and fell still lower, dizzy and faint. Again and again it climbed and tumbled – nine brave attempts were counted. The King felt sure that “the foolish thing” would not try any more. But the spider went up once more, steadily, inch by inch, and with a bold little run at the very last pinch it reached its web. “Bravo, bravo!” cried the King. “The spider defied despair; he conquered, and why shouldn’t I?” Bruce braced his mind, tried once more, and that time he did not fail. The poem is a narrative poem with the rhyme scheme ABAB.',
